# Day 14 – AWS NAT Gateway: Private Subnet Outbound Internet Access

> Part of my 7-month self-directed Cloud + DevOps roadmap (Month 4: AWS Networking).

##  Overview
An EC2 instance in a **private subnet** has no public IP, so it cannot reach the internet by default. In this hands-on lab I built a custom VPC from scratch, added a **NAT Gateway** in the public subnet, routed the private subnet through it, and **proved** that the private instance can reach the internet while staying unreachable from outside. Finally, I cleaned up all paid resources.

**Region:** Europe (Stockholm) `eu-north-1`
**Tools:** AWS Management Console, EC2, VPC, EC2 Instance Connect Endpoint

---

## 🏗️Architecture

```
                      Internet
                         |
              Internet Gateway (my-igw)
                         |
        ┌────────────────┴─────────────────┐
        │        VPC: my-vpc 10.0.0.0/16    │
        │                                   │
        │  Public Subnet 10.0.1.0/24        │
        │  Route: 0.0.0.0/0 -> IGW          │
        │  [ NAT Gateway + Elastic IP ]     │
        │            ^                      │
        │            | 0.0.0.0/0 -> NAT     │
        │  Private Subnet 10.0.2.0/24       │
        │  [ private-ec2 (no public IP) ]   │
        └───────────────────────────────────┘
```

**Analogy:** The VPC is a housing society. The public block faces the main road (IGW). The private block is at the back. The NAT Gateway is the gate guard: residents of the back block can go out through the guard, but outsiders cannot walk in.

